description:
Three sites use the pre-1.65 `match Option { Some(v) => v, None => return Err(...) }` pattern where `let-else` (stable since 1.65, workspace MSRV is 1.88) reads more directly. The crate already uses `let-else` once in `lib.rs:310` so the convention is established.

Clearest case — `memory.rs:664-671` in `update_object`:

    let mut current = match existing {
        Some(v) => v,
        None => {
            return Err(BackendSetError::SetError(SetError::new(
                SetErrorType::NotFound,
            )))
        }
    };

→ `let-else`:

    let Some(mut current) = existing else {
        return Err(BackendSetError::SetError(SetError::new(
            SetErrorType::NotFound,
        )));
    };

Saves 4 lines, eliminates a no-op pattern arm, and makes the early-return intent obvious. clippy::single_match_else flags this.

Secondary sites:
- `task.rs:336-340`, `task_list.rs:236-240`, `task_notification.rs:125-129` — `let id_str = match id_val.as_str() { Some(s) => s.to_owned(), None => continue }`. These have a 'continue' comment explaining the None arm is unreachable (validated above). The match style preserves the comment placement; let-else still works but loses the comment context unless re-attached. Judgment call.
- `memory.rs:720-738` in `destroy_object` — the Some arm has substantial logic (bump_state, change_log_mut, recompute), so the early-return-on-None let-else flip would also work but is a heavier rewrite.

What goes wrong if it stays as-is: nothing functionally — both forms compile to the same code. The argument is reader-experience: a serious Rust practitioner reading the `match` pattern in 2026 expects let-else, especially when one arm is purely an early-return.

Strict scope of this finding: just `memory.rs:664-671` (the cleanest case). The other sites are mentioned for context but I'd defer them to a sweep if the workspace adopts let-else as a canonical convention.
